PR #1267 is KS-1295. PR #1269 is KS-1182. PR #1272 is KS-849. PR #1274 is KS-934.

#1267 KS-1295 (T1). The claim: store()'s `if (!isDbAvailable()) return;` becomes one WARN naming credentialId and a reason, then returns; loadFromDb()'s identical early return is deliberately untouched ("bare silent returns 2 -> 1"); refusing the write is NOT built. Cells W1 (DB unavailable: exactly one WARN with credentialId and reason; no SQL; still retrievable from memory), W2 (DB available: no such WARN AND the INSERT issued with the expected parameters — the control), W3 (two stores, two WARNs, in order). RED PROOF (mandatory, YOUR worktree): revert credentialRepo.ts's store() branch to the parent's bytes -> W1 and W3 RED with their assertion text, W2 GREEN (it asserts an absence that holds on both sides — say whether its INSERT conjunct makes it discriminating); restore by bytes. RUNTIME: call the REAL store() from the built module with isDbAvailable forced false, capture the logger's real output stream, and quote the line — LOG-CONTENT: what exactly is serialised (only credentialId and reason, or the credential object, subject DID or claims too?), at which level, and is the id itself sensitive under the service's own logging rules (READ the vc-issuer logger and any redaction list). Plus the declared overlap above.

#1269 KS-1182 (T1). The claim: delegate on headersSent; status = integer 400-599 else 500 (err.status ?? err.statusCode); err.headers applied for scalar values; err.message echoed only when expose !== false and never on a 5xx. 14 cells (H1-H5, H9, H12, five controls, R1 over a REAL express app on 127.0.0.1). RED PROOF (mandatory): errorHandler.ts at the parent's bytes -> 11 of 14 red and exactly the three controls green, each red named by title and assertion text; restore by bytes. RUNTIME (a real express app from the HEAD's own createApp() or the handler mounted exactly as app.ts mounts it, on 127.0.0.1 only, one process per row as the KS-844 gate did): H5 NaN end-to-end — the process must answer 500 and stay alive (at the parent it exits 1: measure both); HDR-THROW — err.headers {"Bad Name": "x"} and {"X-Ok": "a\r\nInjected: 1"}: node's setHeader THROWS on both (drafter's live probe: ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN and ERR_INVALID_CHAR, nodeprobe_1.out), inside the error handler — measure what the client receives, whether the process survives, whether a partial header set leaks, and whether the original error is masked; HDR-ON-500 — err.headers is applied even when the status was coerced to 500 from a garbage status (finalhandler applies err.headers only for a 4xx/5xx err.status) — measure and rule; `err.status` invalid but `err.statusCode` valid (e.g. status 200, statusCode 404): `??` picks 200 -> 500, not 404 — measure and say whether that is the intended reading. UNREACHABLE-TODAY: the PR says no producer in demo-service sets these statuses or calls next(err) — re-grep it yourself (READ) and grade the claim; a guard against a future producer is legitimate scope, but say what reaches the handler today.

#1272 KS-849 (T1, a runtime product change in KYC). The claim: both mock timers re-read the verification (dbGetVerification) inside the callback and mutate what is in the database NOW; the document is found BY ID in the re-read object. THE KNOWN LIMIT, DECLARED AND TICKETED (grade it as disclosed scope, NEVER as a defect of #1272): at the 3000 ms auto-approve timer the re-read protects every field the timer does not own, but status and currentLevel are still overwritten — an admin REJECTION inside the window still becomes 'approved'; cell S3 pins exactly that, with a comment saying it is not fixed; it is ticketed as KS-1327 (Backlog), "kyc verification writes are a whole-row read-modify-write". KS-1327-LIMIT: you grade that S3 pins the limit HONESTLY (it asserts the overwrite, not its absence) and that the PR body and the code comment say the same thing; you do NOT grade #1272 NO GO for the limit KS-1327 carries. Cells S1 (selfie inside the 1500 ms window: RED at develop, with a VACUITY CONTROL that the document timer really fired), S2 (selfie after the window: green both sides), S3 (reviewer's notes across the 3 s timer: RED at develop). RED PROOF (mandatory): kyc/src/index.ts at the parent's bytes -> S1 and S3 red with their assertion text, S2 green; restore by bytes. MOCK-UPSERT-COLUMNS: the cells assert on the PERSISTED ROW through a mock of ../db that "models the real ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET column list exactly" — compare the mock's column list with dbSaveVerification's SQL at the head, column by column (the drafter READ the SQL's DO UPDATE SET as: ['checks', 'completed_at', 'current_level', 'documents', 'liveness_completed', 'liveness_score', 'overall_score', 'review_notes', 'reviewed_by', 'risk_level', 'status']); a column the SQL updates and the mock does not (or the reverse) is a finding. SWALLOWED-SAVE-ERROR: both timers end `})().catch(() => {})` — a failed re-read or save is silent, and the 'approved (mock)' log line now fires only after a successful save (it fired unconditionally before): measure both with a rejected dbSaveVerification and rule. Name the mock surface honestly (../db, ../subjectDeks, four @secuura/shared functions including authenticate): this file says NOTHING about authorisation. Also run kyc's own suite and report KS-1328 (db.retry.test.ts over vitest's 5 s default under fleet load; Backlog) as the known timing class if it reds — record `uptime` and re-run it ONCE.

#1274 KS-934 (T1). The claim: `LIMIT $2` (TEAMS_NOTIFY_MAX_ROWS 50) with `ORDER BY created_at ASC`, an aggregate deadline (TEAMS_NOTIFY_DEADLINE_MS 10 000), each call given min(PER_ROW 2 000, remaining), and the un-attempted remainder REPORTED (`skipped`, `deadlineExceeded` added to the 200 body). Cells N1 (LIMIT present: RED at develop), N2 (slow peers cannot hold past the deadline; remainder reported: RED at develop, 10048 ms), N3 (no per-call timeout exceeds the remaining budget: RED at develop), CONTROL (a small fast set delivers every row: green both sides). RED PROOF (mandatory): m365-integration/src/index.ts at the parent's bytes -> N1, N2, N3 red with their assertion text, CONTROL green; restore by bytes. STUBBED-GUARD: safeOutboundRequest is STUBBED in the cells (the real guard refuses loopback) — so the cells prove the ROUTE's obligations only; grade that the stub records the timeoutMs it is handed and that nothing in the file claims the guard honours it. ENV-NAN and ENV-ZERO: the three bounds are `Number(process.env.X || default)`; the drafter's live node probe (nodeprobe_1.out) reads Number("abc") = NaN, `NaN <= 0` false (the deadline never trips), Math.min(2000, NaN) = NaN, and setTimeout(fn, NaN) fires after 1 ms (node's TimeoutNaNWarning) — so a garbage DEADLINE or PER_ROW env makes every row "fail" at ~1 ms, and a garbage MAX_ROWS sends `LIMIT NaN` to Postgres; "0" is a truthy string, so DEADLINE=0 skips every row and MAX_ROWS=0 sends LIMIT 0. Measure each through the REAL route (the query stubbed at the module boundary as the cells do) and rule: fail-safe, fail-loud, or silent. SCHEMA-SOURCES: `ORDER BY created_at` needs the column in the schema the product DEPLOYS — the drafter READ it present in docker/init/06-m365-tables.sql and in api-gateway's startup-migrations.ts, and found NO migrations/ file naming svc_teams_webhooks; name which source builds the table the service runs against and say whether both agree (BUILD THE SCHEMA THE PRODUCT DEPLOYS). SPEC-SHAPE: POST /api/teams/notify's 200 is published as M365SuccessSchema (m365-integration.openapi.ts; docs/openapi/secuura-api.yaml) — READ whether the two additive fields are within that schema or undeclared, and say what a strict consumer sees; legs 3/4/8 (served-spec consistency) are NOT run, so this is READ ONLY.

NO STACK, NO DOCKER. Every runtime probe in this kit runs in YOUR OWN worktree with the service's own test tooling or a plain node process, listening on 127.0.0.1 only on an ephemeral port (port 0), with every outbound call and every DB call stubbed at the module boundary exactly as the PR's cells do — never a container, never a named port, NEVER connect to the native Postgres on :5432, and never an outbound request off the box. State for each probe what is real and what is stubbed.

THE RED PROOFS — RE-RUN, RED AT BASE AND GREEN AT HEAD, in YOUR OWN clone (a worktree per PR from refs/pull/<n>/head, plus a worktree of the launch develop for the merged-tree runs). Plant exactly as the seat did (READ its arm JSON first: ks1295-armR.json, ks1182-armR.json, ks849-armR.json, ks934-armR.json and their arm0 twins), in YOUR worktree only; name every red cell by title and assertion text; RESTORE BYTE-IDENTICALLY — restore by bytes (`git diff --exit-code` rc 0 and the blob re-hashed equal); assert the green set after == the green set before the plant (the whole file, not a filtered subset — say the file's cell count before and after). A tamper that does not apply is a FAIL of the arm, never a pass. A red proof must NAME WHY it went red (the assertion text, not a count). A proof you did not re-run is a claim with an author, not evidence.

